WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.081 --> 00:00:08.806
St. Petersburg is without a doubt one of the world's most interesting cities.

2
00:00:09.666 --> 00:00:16.031
Located only a few degrees south of the Arctic Circle, 640 kilometers northwest of Moscow, it is the

3
00:00:16.131 --> 00:00:18.052
second-largest city in Russia.

4
00:00:18.773 --> 00:00:22.595
Many people consider it the cultural capital of Russia—and for good reason.

5
00:00:23.276 --> 00:00:29.240
It is a city of great architectural, cultural, and historical significance, and unlike any other city in the

6
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.374
world. It is hard to believe that a city that is so much a part of Russian history

7
00:00:33.714 --> 00:00:36.195
has been in existence for only three hundred years.

8
00:00:36.855 --> 00:00:42.618
When Czar Peter the Great came to power in 1682, he was intent on modernizing Russia.

9
00:00:43.438 --> 00:00:48.381
Having spent a good deal of his youth in Europe, he dreamed of making his Russian homeland more

10
00:00:48.401 --> 00:00:49.901
like the countries of Western Europe.

11
00:00:50.982 --> 00:00:56.252
After he captured the area near what is now known as the Gulf of Finland from Sweden, he

12
00:00:56.292 --> 00:00:57.833
immediately started to build St.

13
00:00:57.873 --> 00:01:01.854
Petersburg on the delta where the Neva River flows into the Gulf of Finland.

14
00:01:02.614 --> 00:01:08.296
He chose that specific spot because it has water access to the Baltic Sea and the Atlantic Ocean.

15
00:01:08.856 --> 00:01:12.297
For this reason, the city is often called Russia's "window to Europe."

16
00:01:12.977 --> 00:01:19.659
On May 16, 1703, Peter positioned the stones of the Peter and Paul Fortress, the first structure in

17
00:01:19.699 --> 00:01:20.539
St. Petersburg.

18
00:01:21.159 --> 00:01:23.380
But the city's location brought certain problems.

19
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:24.540
Building St.

20
00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.502
Petersburg was difficult because the site is a swampy river delta where the many tributaries of the Neva

21
00:01:30.562 --> 00:01:31.662
flow into the gulf.

22
00:01:32.302 --> 00:01:37.364
The Neva sometimes floods, causing widespread death and destruction over the years.

23
00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:43.666
In addition, the severe northern climate with its long, dark winters resulted in the deaths of many workers.

24
00:01:44.346 --> 00:01:49.988
For all these reasons, people weren't eager to help Peter build this city, so he resorted to using

25
00:01:50.048 --> 00:01:50.728
forced labor.

26
00:01:51.796 --> 00:01:53.738
Although the czar had wanted to make St.

27
00:01:53.818 --> 00:01:58.802
Petersburg look like a Western European city, it soon developed a unique appearance of its own.

28
00:01:59.523 --> 00:02:04.547
Peter had originally imagined that people would travel in boats floating on the canals, as they do in

29
00:02:04.587 --> 00:02:08.710
Venice and Amsterdam, two European cities that he wanted to imitate.

30
00:02:09.531 --> 00:02:15.956
But after his death, people began to focus more on building bridges and streets instead, which encouraged travel

31
00:02:16.196 --> 00:02:17.597
on land more than on water.

32
00:02:18.318 --> 00:02:22.990
Although most of the planned water routes were abandoned, many remain—the reason that St.

33
00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.932
Petersburg is also known as the "Venice of the North."

34
00:02:26.572 --> 00:02:34.037
To make passage possible, the city boasts an astonishing 342 bridges, including many drawbridges across the Neva that

35
00:02:34.097 --> 00:02:35.839
allow large ships to come upriver.

36
00:02:36.859 --> 00:02:38.600
In addition to its bridges, St.

37
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:44.445
Petersburg has so many palaces, each one unique in style and design, that it is often called "the

38
00:02:44.485 --> 00:02:45.445
city of palaces."

39
00:02:46.426 --> 00:02:51.729
One of the most renowned is the rulers' Winter Palace, now a part of the State Hermitage Museum.

40
00:02:52.389 --> 00:02:57.691
This complex, which consists of the Winter Palace and five other buildings on the banks of the Neva,

41
00:02:58.292 --> 00:03:02.753
is home to one of the world's finest collections of Russian, Asian, and Western European art.

42
00:03:03.514 --> 00:03:09.476
The Winter Palace is astounding both inside and out; the green and white three-story building takes up an

43
00:03:09.536 --> 00:03:14.538
entire block, features many classical columns, and has sculptures perched on its roof.

44
00:03:15.419 --> 00:03:24.184
Seeing its 1,057 rooms—filled with marble, chandeliers, gold, and more than three million paintings and artifacts—would take a

45
00:03:24.224 --> 00:03:25.405
visitor more than a week.

46
00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:27.087
The rulers of St.

47
00:03:27.127 --> 00:03:30.449
Petersburg supported the performing arts as well as the visual arts.

48
00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:37.435
Many of the enduring Russian symphonies and operas, such as Mussorgsky's Boris Godunov and Tchaikovsky's Queen of Spades,

49
00:03:37.755 --> 00:03:38.456
premiered in St.

50
00:03:38.496 --> 00:03:40.417
Petersburg's Mariinsky Theater.

51
00:03:40.998 --> 00:03:46.162
The Mariinsky Theater is also home to the Mariinsky Ballet Company, one of the most famous ballet troupes

52
00:03:46.202 --> 00:03:46.763
in history.

53
00:03:47.543 --> 00:03:50.405
St. Petersburg also has many magnificent churches.

54
00:03:51.126 --> 00:03:52.867
One distinguished edifice, St.

55
00:03:53.027 --> 00:03:58.972
Isaac's Cathedral, has one of the world's largest gilded domes—it is covered in more than one hundred kilograms

56
00:03:59.032 --> 00:03:59.472
of gold.

57
00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:05.597
Another, named the Church of Our Savior on Spilled Blood, was erected as a memorial to Czar Alexander

58
00:04:05.897 --> 00:04:11.461
II on the very spot where he was assassinated in 1881 by a young man who threw a

59
00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:14.923
bomb into his carriage as the czar drove through the streets of St.

60
00:04:14.943 --> 00:04:22.537
Petersburg. This structure typifies the colorful mosaics and onion domes that are characteristic of Russian architecture.

61
00:04:23.577 --> 00:04:23.998
One of St.

62
00:04:24.038 --> 00:04:30.762
Petersburg's most recognizable monuments is the bronze statue of Peter the Great on horseback, posed on top of

63
00:04:30.822 --> 00:04:31.923
a huge piece of granite.

64
00:04:32.583 --> 00:04:36.786
Unveiled in 1782, the statue has become an icon of the city.

65
00:04:37.507 --> 00:04:42.290
The statue plays a key role in "The Bronze Horseman," a famous epic poem published in 1833 by

66
00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:46.597
Alexander Pushkin, Russia's most revered poet.

67
00:04:47.478 --> 00:04:53.082
The poem features a peasant who loses everything during one of the Neva's devastating floods and who comes

68
00:04:53.142 --> 00:04:56.824
to believe that he is being chased around the city by the statue of Peter the Great.

69
00:04:57.785 --> 00:05:02.368
In fact, the statue is now called The Bronze Horseman, in homage to the poem.

70
00:05:02.888 --> 00:05:08.072
St. Petersburg has also inspired many other writers and been the setting for many of their works.

71
00:05:09.113 --> 00:05:16.530
St. Petersburg's bitterly cold and dark winters prompted Nikolai Gogol to set his famous short story "The Overcoat"

72
00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.512
during these long, snowy nights.

73
00:05:19.192 --> 00:05:25.977
Conversely, the hot, humid summers, when the sun barely dips below the horizon at night (a phenomenon known

74
00:05:26.097 --> 00:05:31.241
as "the white nights") were the setting for Fyodor Dostoevsky's famous novel Crime Punishment.

75
00:05:31.942 --> 00:05:35.885
St. Petersburg also has reminders of its more recent and tragic history.

76
00:05:36.505 --> 00:05:40.764
During World War II, the Nazis laid siege to Leningrad—as St.

77
00:05:40.804 --> 00:05:48.911
Petersburg was called by Russia's communist leaders from the 1920s until 1991—on September 8, 1941.

78
00:05:49.531 --> 00:05:54.996
Thousands of people there died of starvation because Hitler and the German army prevented nearly all supplies from

79
00:05:55.056 --> 00:05:55.736
reaching the city.

80
00:05:56.597 --> 00:06:00.060
Many people were reduced to eating rats in order to survive.

81
00:06:00.841 --> 00:06:06.105
Others froze to death during terribly cold winters, when temperatures reached as low as negative forty degrees Celsius.

82
00:06:06.986 --> 00:06:08.727
A plaque on one of the columns of St.

83
00:06:08.907 --> 00:06:15.944
Isaac's Cathedral serves as a memorial to the 660,000 people who died during the nine-hundred-day siege.

84
00:06:16.825 --> 00:06:18.626
It is impossible to walk around St.

85
00:06:18.666 --> 00:06:23.429
Petersburg without encountering evidence of its historical and artistic heritage.

86
00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:30.194
The artists, musicians, and writers who have lived here throughout its history have embraced the best of Europe,

87
00:06:30.674 --> 00:06:34.497
as Peter the Great intended, and their own distinct Russian culture as well.

88
00:06:35.217 --> 00:06:41.444
St. Petersburg has undoubtedly exceeded the expectations of its founder, who would be proud that the city everyone

89
00:06:41.504 --> 00:06:45.651
told him could not be built is now one of the most amazing cities in the world.
